WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.571
How can you check what an AI really did?

2
00:00:03.071 --> 00:00:06.310
You ask an AI a question, and it gives you an answer.

3
00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:08.802
But which model really produced it?

4
00:00:08.802 --> 00:00:11.244
And was anything changed along the way?

5
00:00:11.544 --> 00:00:14.307
Today, you take the operator's word for it.

6
00:00:15.107 --> 00:00:17.837
Why not just run it again and compare?

7
00:00:17.837 --> 00:00:21.645
Because computers don't always agree with themselves.

8
00:00:21.945 --> 00:00:23.526
Think of a grocery bill.

9
00:00:23.526 --> 00:00:27.149
Add the items in a different order, rounding as you go,

10
00:00:27.149 --> 00:00:29.388
and the total can shift by a cent.

11
00:00:29.688 --> 00:00:31.116
Computers do the same.

12
00:00:31.116 --> 00:00:35.596
Different chips add in a different order, so one model, on one input,

13
00:00:35.596 --> 00:00:37.933
can give slightly different numbers.

14
00:00:38.233 --> 00:00:41.739
Nobody can say if that is noise, or a changed model.

15
00:00:42.839 --> 00:00:46.432
CIS-2 is an open standard that fixes this.

16
00:00:46.432 --> 00:00:52.593
It is a written specification that pins down every one of those choices.

17
00:00:52.893 --> 00:00:59.852
Think of a recipe so exact that it fixes the order of every step and how to round every measurement.

18
00:00:59.852 --> 00:01:02.983
Any cook, in any kitchen, gets the same cake.

19
00:01:03.883 --> 00:01:08.350
When a model runs under CIS-2, it produces a receipt:

20
00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:11.046
a short fingerprint of its work.

21
00:01:11.346 --> 00:01:17.522
At every step, the model scores about forty-nine thousand possible next words.

22
00:01:17.522 --> 00:01:21.797
CIS-2 folds all of those numbers into one fingerprint.

23
00:01:22.097 --> 00:01:26.002
Change the weights, the input, or one bit of the arithmetic,

24
00:01:26.002 --> 00:01:28.540
and the fingerprint changes completely.

25
00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.735
A recipe is good if a stranger can follow it.

26
00:01:32.735 --> 00:01:35.150
So we tested the text on its own:

27
00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:39.542
separate builds, made from the spec alone, in Rust and in C.

28
00:01:39.842 --> 00:01:45.311
They never saw each other's code, and both matched on Intel and ARM chips.

29
00:01:45.611 --> 00:01:49.033
The test also caught a mistake in our own write-up:

30
00:01:49.033 --> 00:01:51.180
one step was in the wrong order.

31
00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:54.401
We fixed the text, and every build then matched.

32
00:01:55.401 --> 00:01:57.882
You don't have to take our word for it.

33
00:01:57.882 --> 00:01:59.918
The verifier is public and free.

34
00:02:00.218 --> 00:02:05.918
One command downloads the pinned model, checks every file against a known hash,

35
00:02:05.918 --> 00:02:08.299
builds the verifier, and runs it.

36
00:02:08.599 --> 00:02:12.489
It compares every fingerprint with the published one.

37
00:02:12.489 --> 00:02:14.984
If they all match, you get a PASS.

38
00:02:14.984 --> 00:02:17.332
No account, no special hardware.

39
00:02:17.632 --> 00:02:22.671
And if even one byte of the model file is changed, it refuses to run.

40
00:02:23.671 --> 00:02:26.914
Why fingerprint all the numbers, not just the words?

41
00:02:26.914 --> 00:02:31.591
Because the words can stay the same while the model underneath has changed.

42
00:02:31.891 --> 00:02:36.962
In our own tests on a separate, larger model, we corrupted it in small ways.

43
00:02:36.962 --> 00:02:42.567
Comparing the text missed one hundred and eighty-two of two hundred and forty-seven.

44
00:02:42.567 --> 00:02:44.769
The full fingerprint missed none.

45
00:02:45.069 --> 00:02:47.513
Nudge one row out of fifty thousand:

46
00:02:47.513 --> 00:02:50.364
comparing text caught none of forty tries.

47
00:02:50.364 --> 00:02:52.605
The fingerprint caught all forty.

48
00:02:53.605 --> 00:02:59.061
For the main published test, a small open model, a short prompt, and sixteen tokens,

49
00:02:59.061 --> 00:03:01.140
there is one pinned fingerprint.

50
00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:07.033
It was reproduced on Intel chips, a cloud server chip, an ARM phone,

51
00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:10.240
and an NVIDIA Tesla P100 graphics card.

52
00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:12.870
Now, what it does not do.

53
00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:16.897
It does not tell you an answer is right, safe, or fair.

54
00:03:16.897 --> 00:03:20.923
A model can be perfectly repeatable and still be wrong.

55
00:03:21.223 --> 00:03:27.052
Today it covers full-precision numbers and greedy decoding, on small open models,

56
00:03:27.052 --> 00:03:29.211
mostly on ordinary processors.

57
00:03:29.511 --> 00:03:32.392
And every run so far was done by us.

58
00:03:32.392 --> 00:03:35.514
No outside party has reproduced it yet.

59
00:03:35.814 --> 00:03:37.558
So we publish a challenge:

60
00:03:37.558 --> 00:03:41.515
if you can make it differ, tell us, and we will credit you.

61
00:03:42.315 --> 00:03:45.391
CIS-2 is open under the Apache license.

62
00:03:45.391 --> 00:03:52.175
The specification, the verifiers, and the test vectors are on GitHub and Hugging Face.

63
00:03:52.175 --> 00:03:55.646
Run it, break it, and tell us what you find.

